C = dir(40), D = dir(10);\ndraw(A--D--C--A--B--C);\nlabel(\"$A$\",A,W); label(\"$B$\",B,N); label(\"$C$\",C,E); label(\"$D$\",D,E);\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"127b1ffef7a3bdec41382bbd4863df29f19b9f3d483fb27276d96a063b34fcc0"},"seconds":21.51559066772461,"seed":616522156200024338,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the measure of angle \\(CDA\\), we need to understand the given isosceles triangle \\(ABC\\) and the circle that surrounds it. However, the problem setup seems to have an implicit assumption that points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) are on a circle, and we need to find the measure of angle \\(CDA\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\( \\angle BAC = \\angle BCA = 35^\\circ \\)\n- Since \\( \\angle BAC = \\angle BCA \\), triangle \\(ABC\\) is isosceles with \\(AB = BC\\).\n\nFirst, we find the measure of \\( \\angle ABC \\) in triangle \\(ABC\\):\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle BCA = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 110^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, since points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) are on the circle, we use the fact that the angles subtended by the same arc at the circumference are equal. Specifically, \\(\\angle ABC\\) and \\(\\angle ADC\\) are both angles subtended by the arc \\(AC\\).\n\nSince \\(\\angle ABC = 110^\\circ\\), the corresponding angle \\(\\angle ADC\\) (or \\(\\angle CDA\\)) must also be \\(110^\\circ\\) because they subtend the same arc \\(AC\\).\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\(CDA\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{110^\\circ}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{110^\\circ}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
